Date: Wed, 29 Jul 2026 12:41:39 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Apply SVG2 geometry properties from CSS, including calc()

cx, cy, r, rx, ry, x, y, width and height became presentation attributes
in SVG2, so a stylesheet may set them: `circle { r: 65px }` is valid and
WPT's svg/geometry reftests are built on it. is_presentation() predates
that, so every such declaration was dropped: shapes styled only by CSS
rendered at their initial geometry, or not at all.

They are also added to is_non_inheritable(): is_inheritable() is defined
as is_presentation() minus that list, so without it an ancestor's
`style="r: 10px"` would cascade a radius onto every descendant shape.

`d` is excluded on purpose. As a property it takes path("...") function
syntax, which the attribute value parser does not accept.

Values the length parser cannot handle (calc(), auto) are dropped as
before, leaving the initial geometry. For a required dimension that
means the shape is not rendered, matching the spec's treatment of an
invalid value.

SVG2 geometry attributes and properties take CSS <length-percentage>,
which includes calc(). svgtypes has no calc support, so the typed Length
parse failed and the whole value was dropped: shapes written as
cx="calc(80px + 60% - 2em)" (the WPT geometry suite's idiom) kept their
initial geometry and vanished.

convert_calc resolves the linear subset: additive chains of dimension
terms, and products/quotients of a dimension with plain numbers. Each
dimension resolves eagerly through convert_length, so percent keeps its
per-attribute viewport-axis rule and em/ex keep font-size resolution,
all in the one existing place. Non-linear input (dimension * dimension,
division by a dimension, nested parens, var()) returns None and keeps
the drop-to-default behaviour.

Hooked where lengths are read: SvgNode::convert_length for the common
path, and resolve_rx_ry's separate typed reads, where a calc value
resolves to a px Length ahead of the auto/negative handling so ellipse
rx/ry auto-fallback still works.
